			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3780323">
									

<p>I think what this article is missing is two angles.</p>
<p>You've got a person whose anomaly requires, if you want to keep her in one place, that she never be left alone. So angle 1 is lack of privacy.</p>
<p>Angle two is that they have to keep someone with her at all times. <em>All times</em>. Doesn't matter if it's a D-class or someone more trustworthy and better trained who specializes in humanoid anomaly psychology, the Foundation essentially has to incarcerate a second person to keep one contained. A story (because SCPs are stories) focused on how this anomaly affects everyone involved at the personal level would be a lot more compelling than &quot;you have to watch her or she can get out&quot;.</p>

